Analysis Module: Visualizing Personal Financial Data
Author: [seif]
Course/Project: Smart Expense Tracker

ABSTRACT
This document outlines the design, architecture, and implementation of the Analysis module for the Smart Expense Tracker application. While the broader application utilizes Jetpack Compose, this specific module was developed using the traditional Android View system (XML architecture). The module aims to provide users with accurate, visual summaries of their transactional data. It utilizes the Model-View-ViewModel (MVVM) architectural pattern to connect to a shared data repository, process expense calculations, and render visual indicators via a Pie Chart and categorized lists. The implementation emphasizes UI component reusability and robust error handling.

Keywords: Android, Kotlin, MVVM, XML, Data Visualization, Financial Tracking

1. INTRODUCTION
The Smart Expense Tracker is a native Android application designed to help users manage their personal finances. The application features several modules, including a Dashboard, Transactions, Budget, and Reports. The primary objective of the Analysis module is to transform raw transaction data into meaningful insights, allowing users to understand their spending patterns.

To demonstrate architectural flexibility and adherence to the project's constraints, the Analysis module is implemented using traditional Android Views and XML-based layouts, contrasting with the Jetpack Compose framework used elsewhere in the app.

2. SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE
2.1 Data Flow and Repository Connection
The module follows the MVVM architecture. The AnalysisViewModel acts as the bridge between the UI and the underlying data. It connects directly to the shared Repository to fetch the user's transaction history. The ViewModel processes this raw data off the main thread using Kotlin Coroutines, ensuring the UI remains responsive.

2.2 State Management and Edge Cases
The ViewModel exposes an AnalysisState data class via LiveData. This state encapsulates:

Total spending calculation.

Category-wise spending summaries.

Loading states.

Error messages.

The module robustly handles edge cases. If the repository returns an empty list, the UI gracefully displays a "No expenses recorded" message. If a calculation error occurs, an error message is surfaced to the user rather than allowing an application crash.

3. IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS
3.1 Reusable UI Components
To adhere to best practices, UI components were designed to be modular and independent of the Analysis screen. A programmatic custom View, CategorySpendingItemView, was developed. This component encapsulates the layout and logic for displaying a single category and its corresponding amount. Because it is independent, it can be seamlessly integrated into other application features (such as the Budget screen) without introducing unnecessary dependencies.

3.2 Data Visualization
To satisfy the requirement for visual indicators, the module integrates the MPAndroidChart library. The AnalysisViewModel passes aggregated category data to the View, which dynamically generates a PieChart. This provides an immediate, intuitive understanding of spending distributions. A RecyclerView, powered by the aforementioned reusable custom view, provides the textual breakdown.

3.3 Shared Theming
The Analysis screen strictly adheres to the application's shared theme and Material Design 3 styling. By utilizing standard Android TextView and LayoutParams that inherit from the Activity's context, the module ensures visual consistency with other screens while avoiding hardcoded styling that could break the global theme.

4. CONCLUSION
The Analysis module successfully fulfills all project requirements using an XML-based architecture. It connects securely to shared data sources, performs necessary financial calculations, visualizes data via charts, and maintains code quality through reusable components and robust error handling.

